Deep Meta — three patterns worth keeping

1. Consumer weakness and oil are now connected nodes

Walmart supplied an unusually clean causal observation. Management specifically cited elevated gasoline prices as consumers pulled back; traffic growth slowed to 1.5% and transaction growth to 1.1%. 

So XOM and WMT shouldn’t be modeled independently:

\text{geopolitical constraint}
\rightarrow
\text{oil}\uparrow
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
XOM\ cashflow\ expectations\uparrow\\
consumer\ discretionary\ income\downarrow\\
inflation\ expectations\uparrow\\
Treasury\ yields\uparrow
\end{cases}

and then:

\text{yields}\uparrow
\rightarrow
\text{long-duration tech valuation pressure}.

One shock is therefore propagating through energy → consumer → bonds → technology.

That’s an Asset Atlas edge worth preserving.

2. AI’s physical-infrastructure derivative just got another confirmation

This is especially interesting after CAT’s failed momentum signal.

Today Deere & Company reported that its construction and forestry segment grew sales 18%, with Reuters explicitly attributing the improvement partly to an AI-driven construction boom. 

