CAT’s stock weakness therefore doesn’t falsify AI → physical infrastructure. It tells us we’d conflated a good thematic causal edge with a good immediate entry price.

That’s an important Goblin distinction:

\boxed{\text{Correct world model}\neq\text{correct trade timing}}

3. The Ouroboros financing loop strengthened

Alibaba reported today that quarterly profit fell 75% while AI infrastructure capex rose 75%; cloud revenue simultaneously increased 45%. 

Combine that with hyperscaler borrowing, high Treasury yields and Nvidia’s increasing involvement in AI infrastructure financing, and the emerging loop becomes:

AI\ demand\uparrow
\rightarrow
capex\uparrow
\rightarrow
financing\ demand\uparrow
\rightarrow
yields/cost\ of\ capital\uparrow

while simultaneously:

AI\ capex\uparrow
\rightarrow
NVDA/CAT/DE/etc.\ revenue\uparrow.

That’s chiral in exactly the useful sense: the same rotation generates propulsion and resistance.

The Goblin should therefore stop treating Treasury yields as merely a macro background variable for NVDA. Cost of capital is becoming an endogenous variable inside the AI buildout itself.

Today’s best new Pattern Card

Theme ≠ Entry.

A powerful secular trend can simultaneously produce:

* excellent suppliers,
* worsening financing conditions,
* temporarily terrible stock entries,
* second-order winners,
* consumer losers,
* and eventually its own limiting feedback.

That is already transferring beyond our original six stocks.

And it gives us a sharper Moneyball search instruction:

Don’t ask only “who benefits from the trend?” Search for who benefits from the bottlenecks the trend creates—and whose price has not yet incorporated that benefit.

That is where I’d aim the Goblin’s next pulse.